Original commit body from deb4b444521c064ec632f4f4cf6d35dd9462d186:

defprotocol/defimpl were unmodelled: a protocol's bodiless def heads
and impl defs were emitted as bare-named functions with no module, and
a defimpl nested in defmodule Outer was attributed to Outer.size
though its module is Size.Outer. No module symbol, no type
relationship, no dispatch.

Identity: defprotocol P is a module (nested like defmodule); its def
heads are callback-shaped symbols (abstract, meta["arity"],
callback_stable_id). defimpl P, for: T is the absolute module P.T owning
its defs; for: [A, B] yields one impl per target; no for: / __MODULE__
take the enclosing module; P and T are alias-expanded (file aliases
plus nested-module auto-aliases). An unreadable impl (unquote target,
quote template) owns nothing rather than a bare name. All three
definers share _enclosing_module_names.

Edges reuse hypergumbo_core.behaviours via _behaviour_edges:
implements P.T -> P (or the external unresolved target) and protocol
function -> impl dispatches_to by name and arity. Impls of protocols
outside the repo are rooted by their module, derived from "implements
a protocol the repo does not define". Protocol functions are qualified
call targets.

Behaviour callbacks now carry meta["arity"] and match implementations
through overload_arity.narrow_by_arity (INV-dital): 10 false
callback edges dropped on phoenix.

